Drain the terminal's pending replies before each test's reset.

A test that fails part-way through a read leaves the rest of the terminal's
answer sitting in the pty, and esctest never takes it out again: the next
test's `reset()` reads that stale byte where it expected a CSI, fails, and
leaves *its* reply behind in turn. One assertion failure therefore fails every
test after it, and the log fills with `Read f (0x66), expected CSI` in tests
that have nothing to do with the one that broke.

This is a property of the harness rather than of any terminal — the trigger is
only that an answer differed from what a test asserted — and it hides the
result the suite was run to get. `escio.Drain()` throws away whatever is
already readable and `reset()` calls it before writing anything, so every test
starts against a quiet pty.

Not yet reported upstream; see README.md.

diff --git a/esctest/escio.py b/esctest/escio.py
index c12d05a..55f1cfa 100644
--- a/esctest/escio.py
+++ b/esctest/escio.py
@@ -30,6 +30,17 @@ def Write(s, sideChannelOk=True):
     gSideChannel.write(escoding.to_binary(s))
   stdout_fd.write(escoding.to_binary(s))
 
+def Drain():
+  """Throw away anything the terminal has already sent.
+
+  Called at the start of each test's reset so that a reply left unread by a
+  failed assertion cannot desynchronise every test after it.
+  """
+  f = sys.stdin.fileno()
+  while select.select([f], [], [], 0)[0]:
+    if not os.read(f, 4096):
+      return
+
 def SetSideChannel(filename):
   global gSideChannel
   if filename is None:
diff --git a/esctest/esctest.py b/esctest/esctest.py
index 3312bea..89b6cdf 100755
--- a/esctest/esctest.py
+++ b/esctest/esctest.py
@@ -39,6 +39,10 @@ def shutdown():
 
 def reset():
   '''Reset terminal to known state, at the beginning of each unit test.'''
+  # Anything still readable belongs to a test that has already ended, and
+  # leaving it there fails every test after that one. See the patch note.
+  escio.Drain()
+
   esccmd.DECSCL(60 + esc.vtLevel, 1)
 
   escio.use8BitControls = False
